---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro'
import DiagramFigure from '../components/DiagramFigure.astro'
import { samples, NATIVE_COUNT, DETECTED_KEYWORD_COUNT, GOLDEN_CASE_COUNT } from '../data/samples'

const hero = samples.find((s) => s.id === 'flowchart')!
const pie = samples.find((s) => s.id === 'pie')!

const runtimes = [
  {
    name: 'Rust',
    note: 'The runtime crate, with the parse artifact baked into the binary.',
    install: 'cargo add etassia',
    href: 'https://crates.io/crates/etassia',
  },
  {
    name: 'Ruby',
    note: 'The pure-Ruby reference implementation; the etassia-native gem carries the compiled engine.',
    install: 'gem install etassia',
    href: 'https://rubygems.org/gems/etassia',
  },
  {
    name: 'Python',
    note: 'ctypes binding over the three-function C ABI of the shared library.',
    install: 'pip install etassia',
    href: 'https://pypi.org/project/etassia/',
  },
  {
    name: 'JavaScript',
    note: 'The engine compiled to WebAssembly, with no JavaScript-side reformatting.',
    install: 'npm install etassia',
    href: 'https://www.npmjs.com/package/etassia',
  },
]

const syntaxCounts = [
  { value: String(DETECTED_KEYWORD_COUNT), label: 'Mermaid diagram keywords detected' },
  { value: String(NATIVE_COUNT), label: 'diagram types rendered natively by the Rust runtime' },
  { value: GOLDEN_CASE_COUNT.toLocaleString('en-US'), label: 'golden-corpus cases with byte-identical SVG' },
]
---
<Layout>
  <section class="bg-gradient-to-b from-primary-50 dark:from-primary-900/20 to-transparent">
    <div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-16 lg:py-24">
      <div class="max-w-3xl">
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-5xl font-bold tracking-tight text-gray-900 dark:text-white">Etassia</h1>
        <p class="mt-3 text-lg text-primary-700 dark:text-primary-300 italic">
          <em>&#7940;&#954;&#964;&#940;&#963;&#963;&#969;</em> &mdash; drawn up in formation. The diagram language.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300">
          Etassia is a diagram-rendering engine. It reads Mermaid diagram syntax &mdash; flowcharts,
          sequence diagrams, class diagrams, state diagrams, and the chart and C4 families among
          {DETECTED_KEYWORD_COUNT} detected diagram keywords &mdash; and produces SVG output. The engine
          exists as a Rust runtime whose parse artifact is compiled ahead of time and checksummed, and
          every diagram type in the native set is served by a hand-ported renderer. The implementation is
          validated against a golden corpus of {GOLDEN_CASE_COUNT} cases in which the rendered SVG is
          byte-identical to the output of the reference Ruby implementation, which makes the four runtime
          bindings interchangeable.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
          <a href="/playground" class="btn btn-primary">Open the playground</a>
          <a href="/syntax" class="btn btn-secondary">Browse the supported syntaxes</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-12">
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-6">
      {syntaxCounts.map((c) => (
        <div class="card text-center">
          <p class="text-3xl font-bold text-primary-600 dark:text-primary-400">{c.value}</p>
          <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400">{c.label}</p>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-12">
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-2 gap-10 items-start">
      <div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white">One source text produces identical SVG on every runtime</h2>
        <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300">
          Documentation pipelines mix languages. A static-site generator written in Ruby, a
          specification toolchain written in Python, and a documentation server written in Rust or
          JavaScript can each need to render the same diagram source, and a diagram that differs
          between those pipelines is a defect that is difficult to attribute. Etassia addresses this
          problem by keeping one Rust runtime as the rendering engine and exposing it through four
          runtime bindings, and by validating the output of every diagram type against the reference
          implementation rather than against a visual impression of it.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300">
          The four bindings share one native boundary: a C application binary interface (ABI) of
          three functions, <code class="px-1 py-0.5 rounded bg-gray-100 dark:bg-gray-800 text-sm">etassia_render</code>,
          <code class="px-1 py-0.5 rounded bg-gray-100 dark:bg-gray-800 text-sm">etassia_detect</code>, and
          <code class="px-1 py-0.5 rounded bg-gray-100 dark:bg-gray-800 text-sm">etassia_free</code>.
          The Python binding reaches the ABI through ctypes, the WebAssembly build exports the same
          surface over linear memory, and the Ruby compiled-engine gem loads the shared library that
          the etassia-native package ships.
        </p>
        <DiagramFigure
          src={hero.svg}
          alt="A left-to-right flowchart of a decision process, rendered from Mermaid syntax"
          caption={`Rendered from a golden-corpus flowchart source. The SVG is a byte-identical copy of the reference implementation output (${GOLDEN_CASE_COUNT.toLocaleString('en-US')} corpus cases carry this property).`}
        />
      </div>
      <div>
        <DiagramFigure
          src={pie.svg}
          alt="A pie chart of pets adopted by volunteers, rendered from Mermaid syntax"
          caption="A pie chart from the same corpus; the slices, labels and legend are produced by the pie renderer of the Rust runtime."
          maxWidth="36rem"
        />
        <div class="card mt-4">
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white">What the runtime guarantees</h3>
          <ul class="mt-3 space-y-2 text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 list-disc pl-5">
            <li>
              The parse tree comes from a checksummed grammar artifact that is compiled ahead of
              time, so parsing does not depend on a grammar interpreter at run time.
            </li>
            <li>
              Every natively rendered diagram type has a hand-ported renderer, and its SVG is
              byte-identical to the reference Ruby implementation on the golden corpus.
            </li>
            <li>
              A diagram type without a native renderer reports an Unported condition through the
              same error surface, which the bindings translate into a fallback call to the
              reference implementation.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-12">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white">Four runtimes</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 max-w-3xl text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
      Each runtime is distributed through its native registry under the package name
      <code class="px-1 py-0.5 rounded bg-gray-100 dark:bg-gray-800 text-sm">etassia</code>; the
      compiled-engine gem for Ruby is named <code class="px-1 py-0.5 rounded bg-gray-100 dark:bg-gray-800 text-sm">etassia-native</code>.
    </p>
    <div class="mt-6 grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4 gap-6">
      {runtimes.map((r) => (
        <div class="card card-hover">
          <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-2">
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white">{r.name}</h3>
            <a href={r.href} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-xs text-primary-600 dark:text-primary-400 hover:underline">registry</a>
          </div>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">{r.note}</p>
          <pre class="mt-3 rounded-lg bg-gray-900 text-gray-100 text-xs px-3 py-2 overflow-x-auto"><code>{r.install}</code></pre>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-12 pb-20">
    <div class="card">
      <div class="flex flex-col md:flex-row md:items-center md:justify-between gap-4">
        <div>
          <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white">Supported syntaxes</h2>
          <p class="mt-2 max-w-2xl text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
            The detector recognizes {DETECTED_KEYWORD_COUNT} Mermaid diagram keywords, including
            <code class="px-1 rounded bg-gray-100 dark:bg-gray-800 text-sm">graph</code>,
            <code class="px-1 rounded bg-gray-100 dark:bg-gray-800 text-sm">flowchart-elk</code> and the
            five C4 kinds. The Rust runtime renders {NATIVE_COUNT} diagram types natively; the
            remaining detected types return an Unported condition that the bindings translate into a
            fallback call. The syntax page lists every type with its source and its rendered output.
          </p>
        </div>
        <a href="/syntax" class="btn btn-primary shrink-0">See all diagram types</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
